.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH sentinel-scan 1  "scan " 
.SH NAME
sentinel\-scan \- Scan the Git repository at `[PATH]` for secrets (default: the cwd)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsentinel\-scan\fR [\fB\-\-ci\fR] [\fB\-\-offline\fR] [\fB\-\-explain\fR] [\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\fR] [\fB\-\-report\fR] [\fB\-\-fail\-under\fR] [\fB\-\-baseline\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR] [\fB\-\-update\-baseline\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIPATH\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scan the Git repository at `[PATH]` for secrets (default: the cwd)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-ci\fR
Run with hermetic (CI) ignore sources: parent, global, and `.git/info/exclude` ambient ignores are disabled
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable network and cache writes; use any\-age cache only
.TP
\fB\-\-explain\fR
Show one advisory line per finding on terminal output. Advisory\-only: never changes findings, severity, ordering, or the exit code, and is forced off under `\-\-ci` and `\-\-offline`
.TP
\fB\-\-output\fR \fI<OUTPUT>\fR [default: terminal]
Report format: terminal, json, or sarif (default terminal)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
terminal
.IP \(bu 2
json
.IP \(bu 2
sarif
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Exclude files matching a glob from the scan. Repeatable; patterns use `.gitignore` semantics and are matched against repo\-relative paths after `.sentinelignore` and config excludes
.TP
\fB\-\-report\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Write the machine\-readable report to a file instead of stdout
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-under\fR \fI<FAIL_UNDER>\fR
Lowest severity that makes a completed scan exit 1: `critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, or `none` for report\-only (a completed scan always exits 0). Defaults to `low`, so any finding exits 1 — today\*(Aqs behaviour. Also settable as the `fail_under` config key; the flag wins over project config, which wins over user config. Diagnostics, and scans that did not run (exit 2), are unaffected
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
critical
.IP \(bu 2
high
.IP \(bu 2
medium
.IP \(bu 2
low
.IP \(bu 2
none
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Path to a JSON baseline file listing accepted finding fingerprints (see `\-\-update\-baseline` for the format). Findings whose baseline key is listed still report — marked `[accepted]` on terminal, `"accepted": true` in JSON, and an external accepted suppression in SARIF — but never count toward `\-\-fail\-under`. A fingerprint in the file matching no current finding warns once as `baseline\-stale`. Also settable as the `baseline` config key; the flag wins over project config, which wins over user config. Relative project\-config values resolve against the repository root, the flag against the invoking directory. Absent entirely, the scan behaves exactly as today
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR
Skip the XDG cache entirely: perform no cache reads and no cache writes. Findings are the same as a scan with a warm cache. Under `\-\-offline` the cache is the only source of advisory data, so a no\-cache offline scan degrades to `osv\-unavailable` warnings for every dependency pin while secrets findings still report — the combination is allowed and never exits 2 on its own
.TP
\fB\-\-update\-baseline\fR
Regenerate the baseline file from this scan\*(Aqs findings and report against it: the file the `\-\-baseline` flag or the `baseline` config key names is rewritten with every current finding\*(Aqs fingerprint (sorted, deduplicated), stale entries pruned. The write is reported as `baseline\-updated` on stderr; without any baseline path the scan exits 2. This is the only write the tool performs besides the XDG cache
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
[\fIPATH\fR] [default: .]
Directory to scan: resolved against the invoking cwd, then the enclosing Git repository is scanned. The project `sentinel.toml` and `.sentinelignore` come from the target\*(Aqs top level, never the caller\*(Aqs, and `\-\-ci` hermeticity applies to the target walk. Defaults to `.`, so `sentinel scan` scans the cwd unchanged
